WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
How can one cell become two without leaving either new cell with half a chromosome

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.120
set? Watch one blue chromosome through the whole answer.

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.340
Its partners will stay visible, but blue is our guide.

4
00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:20.983
A new cell enters G1, where it grows and does its everyday work.

5
00:00:22.243 --> 00:00:24.663
This busy stretch is interphase, not a rest.

6
00:00:26.103 --> 00:00:29.103
Next comes S phase, when the cell copies its DNA.

7
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.563
G2 prepares the cell to divide.

8
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:36.417
During S phase, every chromosome is copied.

9
00:00:37.697 --> 00:00:42.577
The blue rod becomes two matching blue sister chromatids joined at one centromere.

10
00:00:43.857 --> 00:00:45.257
Its partners copy too.

11
00:00:46.537 --> 00:00:51.637
The DNA amount doubles, but each joined pair still counts as one copied chromosome.

12
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.460
Soon the copied chromosomes will line up across the cell's middle.

13
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.360
Where must the two blue sister chromatids go so both new nuclei receive a complete

14
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.780
set? Hold your answer.

15
00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:13.317
The blue copies must move to opposite poles.

16
00:01:14.697 --> 00:01:17.017
The same is true for every copied chromosome.

17
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:22.917
Copy each chromosome first; separate the copies so each nucleus gets one.

18
00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:28.497
That rule preserves the chromosome set through a typical mitotic division.

19
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.520
M phase begins with mitosis, division of the nucleus.

20
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.040
In prophase, the copied chromosomes coil into thick visible shapes.

21
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.720
Green spindle fibers form.

22
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.780
Near the end, the yellow nuclear envelope breaks down, opening access to the

23
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:48.780
chromosomes.

24
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:54.183
In metaphase, spindle fibers line the copied chromosomes across the middle.

25
00:01:55.503 --> 00:01:57.943
Each sister chromatid faces a different pole.

26
00:01:59.283 --> 00:02:00.403
The row is not the goal.

27
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:05.603
It is the setup that lets matching copies move in opposite directions.

28
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:10.687
In anaphase, the sister chromatids separate at their centromeres.

29
00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:15.827
Spindle fibers pull one copy of every chromosome toward each pole.

30
00:02:17.507 --> 00:02:22.207
Follow blue. One blue copy moves left, while its match moves right.

31
00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:26.467
Each separated chromatid now counts as a chromosome.

32
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.730
In telophase, a complete set reaches each end.

33
00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.250
Yellow nuclear envelopes form, and the chromosomes loosen.

34
00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.530
Mitosis has made two nuclei.

35
00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.570
Cytokinesis often begins in late anaphase or telophase.

36
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.290
It divides the cytoplasm.

37
00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:52.770
An animal cell pinches inward; a plant cell builds a plate outward into a new wall.

38
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.540
Notice what did not happen.

39
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.900
The cell did not split its original chromosomes in half.

40
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.840
It copied every chromosome before separation.

41
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.800
That is why each finished nucleus can receive a complete set, not a random half.
